  <string name="block_rules">Block rules</string>
  <string name="block_rules_desc">One rule per line, written as Field=pattern. The fields are Title, URL, Author, Tag and Content, and the pattern is a regular expression matched anywhere in the field. Begin it with (?i) to ignore case. A matching article is never downloaded, and removing the rule later does not bring it back.</string>
  <string name="allow_rules">Allow rules</string>
  <string name="allow_rules_desc">Written the same way. If there is anything here at all, only articles matching one of these lines are downloaded. A Tag rule is skipped for an article that carries no tags, so a feed without them is not emptied by a Tag rule alone.</string>
  <string name="article_rules_placeholder">Title=(?i)sponsored</string>
  <string name="article_rules_effect">Rules take effect on the next fetch. Articles already here are left alone.</string>
  <string name="rule_error_missing_separator">Line %1$d: no \'=\' in it. This line is ignored.</string>
  <string name="rule_error_unknown_field">Line %1$d: \'%2$s\' is not one of the fields. This line is ignored.</string>
  <string name="rule_error_empty_pattern">Line %1$d: the pattern is empty, which would match everything. This line is ignored.</string>
  <string name="rule_error_invalid_regex">Line %1$d: not a regular expression. This line is ignored.</string>
  <string name="rule_error_too_many_rules">Line %1$d: only the first %2$d rules are used. The rest are ignored.</string>
    <string name="about">About</string>
  <!-- The About dialog -->
  <!-- Heading. %1$s is the version number, e.g. 0.3.2 -->
  <string name="about_title">Feeds %1$s</string>
  <string name="about_what">A reader for feeds and for the smolnet — Gemini, gopher and Spartan. Text on paper, no images, and everything kept on the phone.</string>
  <string name="about_privacy_head">Privacy</string>
  <string name="about_privacy">The app connects only to the feeds, pages and capsules you add, and fetches them directly; that request carries no account and nothing you have written. Some feeds are only served over plain http: those are fetched as the site serves them, with no encryption, so anyone on the network could read the feed text. Only the feed or page you asked for is requested; nothing else is sent. Your feeds and saved articles stay on this phone. Sync with a feed server is off unless you sign in to one in Settings; then the app also talks to that server, and only the one you typed, sending your login, your feeds and what you have read or starred. Its password is kept sealed with the phone\'s keystore. Nothing is sent to the app\'s maker. There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking. Backups and exports go only where you choose to save them. The camera is used only while you scan a QR code, to read it; nothing it sees is kept or sent.</string>
